Raw dev notes -- "Eval-uation Station: Building Trust in RAG"

Okay, so this past week has been a deep dive into making sure our RAG knowledge base pipeline actually *works* and doesn't just hallucinate confidently. The whole point of this project is to build a reliable Q&A system that can answer specific domain questions by pulling from a curated knowledge base, rather than just relying on a general LLM. We're constantly syncing new Q&A pairs from our source (`***.net`) to keep the knowledge fresh, which is a continuous background task.

The big push this period was all about evaluation. I mean, how do you know your RAG is getting better, or even staying good, if you don't have a solid way to measure it? I spent a huge chunk of time building out a daily continuous-regression evaluation framework. This thing is pretty comprehensive now: it includes a 50-question A/B test to compare different models or configurations, plus a quick smoke validation to catch obvious breaks.

One of the coolest, and most challenging, parts was getting the visualization right for the evaluation. I built an HTML side-by-side comparison tool. This isn't just about seeing two answers next to each other; it actually expands the `cited_text` inline, so you can immediately see *what* the RAG system used to formulate its answer. This is crucial for debugging and understanding why an answer might be good or bad. There was a specific fix to make sure we were showing "Rabbi Enbal's actual answer" – which was a bit of a facepalm moment, realizing we weren't comparing against the true ground truth properly at first. Also added a corpus-overlap badge, another visual cue for how well the RAG is leveraging its source material.

Automating this whole evaluation process was another beast. I had to create a wrapper that starts and stops the chatwrapper around the daily run. It's one thing to run an eval manually, another to have it fire off reliably every day without me babysitting it. We also got "real questions" and "ground truth" integrated, which is a huge step up from synthetic or less-curated eval sets. And, of course, an LLM grading pack is ready to go, which should help scale the evaluation process even further.

On the infrastructure and documentation side, I mapped out the drift mechanism (how our data changes over time and how we track it) and an overall automation map for the CLAUDE.md docs. Also made sure the GDrive mirror picks up the split data correctly. It's all about making the system robust and understandable, not just functional.

Honestly, building this evaluation framework has been a grind, but super rewarding. It felt like I was constantly chasing my tail trying to define "good" for an LLM-based system. The struggle to get the ground truth comparison right, and then to visualize it effectively, really highlighted how complex RAG evaluation is. It's not just about accuracy, but about faithfulness, relevance, and citation quality. I learned that you can't just build a RAG and hope for the best; you *have* to build a robust, automated way to continuously test and validate it. It's a huge step in building trust in these AI systems, and it feels good to have laid down such a solid foundation.

Next up: Integrate the LLM grading pack and start running it regularly.
